    "turn_text": "Yeah, some people took what I said in one interview out of contact. I've talked about this quite a bit. I fully support Open Carry in Michigan. I'm a huge supporter of the Second Amendment. I'm a huge supporter of gun rights, and I will protect gun rights 100% as president. I believe very strongly in it. It's a protection for the people, and it's a protection against the government, frankly. So I'm a strong supporter of gun rights. I do think that when people come into the capital with large weapons, they can send the wrong message There are people at home who watch this and maybe put off by it. So those of us who actually care about protecting our rights may be harmed by this because there could be a backlash then. People say, well, what is this? People are opening weapons above legislators who are potentially voting on legislation. This is not right. So you're sending the wrong message just because we have a right and I will fully support that right and I fully support their right. But just because you have a right doesn't mean you should exercise it in a particular way or in every instance. You have to be mindful of how people will respond to you. We have all sorts of rights every day that we don't exercise. We are careful about it or careful about how we exercise it because we want to live together in a society and we want to influence people. We don't want to turn people away.",
