    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88120.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Well, how they react to me will be up to them, but I will reach out a hand to all of them. I've always done that. I've been a very independent Republican in Congress, and then I became a full independent, and now I'm serving as a libertarian in Congress. And at each stage, I've been very happy to work with Republicans and Democrats when we have commonality on an issue. So if we agree on something, of course, we get together. And when we disagree, I'm the kind of person who will sit down with you and spend some time discussing it and we'll try to resolve it if we can and if not we'll be polite and respectful about the issue. I'm not going to hammer people like the way the president does if they don't agree with him on a particular piece of legislation. That's not my style. So yeah, I'll work with people, I'll reach out to people and it's going to have to be a a team effort, you know, the president can't do everything and part of the reason I'm running is to limit the president's power. Congress has to legislate. So I want Congress to do its job. I want Congress to represent the people, unlike the way it works now, where a couple of leaders just come to the White House and negotiate everything directly with the president. I'm going to tell leaders, no, you're not negotiating directly with me. You're going to go back to your chambers the House and Senate and you're going to work with your own legislators so that you can represent the people and then you bring your legislation to me and then I can decide whether I'm going to sign it or veto it and over time they're going to get a sense for where I am on these issues and they will start to adjust accordingly and I'll use the position of the White House to get the message out about what we should be doing but I'm not going to write the legislation for the legislators. I'm not going to force my hand on them. They're going to have to legislate and make decisions for the people.",
