    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88120.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Well, there's essentially lots of things that could be vetoed. One of the things that I see as an immediate potential for a veto would be spending bills. The spending bills are insane. And we can't continue to spend like there's no tomorrow. We have to be concerned about the next generation. And right now what happens is they add more and more money to every bill. Everyone gets a piece of it. When what they call compromise is just each side adding more money. So they say, OK, well, We'll be less fiscally conservative, we'll be reckless, and you guys be more reckless, and we'll call it a deal. And I'm not going to accept that. If they want to compromise, then they can compromise by cutting spending, by finding ways to reduce the size and scope of the federal government. We still have state governments and local governments. If the federal government's not doing something, state and local governments can be involved in it if it's appropriate under those state constitutions. We should allow people to make decisions closer to home. But the advantage of doing it under state law versus federal law is that the state governments have to balance their budgets. The federal government doesn't have to balance its budget right now. So you can force them fiscal sanity by pushing things to the state level where they are probably more appropriate because our federal constitution actually doesn't allow that many It doesn't have that many powers to give into Congress or give into the executive branch. So we can push things back to the state level where they're more appropriate.",
