  {
    "episode_id": 88120,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88120.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 92,
    "turn_text": "I agree. I really hope that people are starting to pay attention because they have nothing else to do at home at this point. So I really hope that people are starting to take the time to look and see what their elected officials are doing. Don't get me wrong, some are doing good things, they're few and far between, and now is the time to truly hold people accountable. We've got, oh, I don't know, six months until elections, it's 180 days. You're gonna have maybe 12 people on your ballot max. We've got 180 days to spend an hour on each of those people and see what are they doing? Are they doing things that align with your values? Are they doing things that directly oppose your values? Who's competing with them? What are their stances? How do their stances align with your values? And really take a look at this and make the decision to get up, go out and vote. And vote your conscience. Vote for the person who you think is going to best represent you. And don't vote based on who you think has a possibility of winning, who has a better shot. Don't create some weird strategy in your head as to how you're going to impact votes. Because that's what's gotten us to where we are. Instead, you need to sit there and say, if I vote my conscience and everybody around me votes their conscience, then we can have true impact in shaping the direction of your town, your county, your state, your federal government, and actually create the future that you envision for your children.",
